    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Where a traditional agency is the right call</h2>
    <p>Many agencies do good work, and the right one is the better choice when:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>You need a full brand redesign.</strong> Weeks of brand identity work, photography, and video call for several specialists.</li>
      <li><strong>You want all your marketing under one roof.</strong> One firm running ads, email, content, social media, and the website adds value when all five work together.</li>
      <li><strong>Many people must approve the work.</strong> Agency project management is built for large teams and complex sign-offs.</li>
      <li><strong>You can spend $25,000 or more up front, plus an ongoing budget.</strong> Agencies price for projects of that size.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where a traditional agency costs you more</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Shared attention.</strong> Agencies juggle 20 to 50 active projects, and the senior designer who pitched you may hand your code to a junior developer or an offshore subcontractor.</li>
      <li><strong>Polished mockups, slower sites.</strong> Many agency projects end with a beautiful design file and a Wix or WordPress build that performs poorly.</li>
      <li><strong>Per-change fees.</strong> At a typical $100 to $200 per update, the small-edit bill by year two often equals a custom-coded site's yearly cost.</li>
      <li><strong>Staff turnover.</strong> When account managers leave, what they knew about your site goes with them, and two years in, you're explaining it to a stranger.</li>
      <li><strong>Speed comes second.</strong> Agency-built service-business sites typically score 50&ndash;75 on Google's speed test, against 95&ndash;100 for custom code, and that gap costs leads every day.</li>
    </ul>

